{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's mechanism is operationally circular for a single evaluation: to distinguish genuine reliability from mere compliance, it embeds a decoy item that is 'objectively irrelevant or counter-intuitive to the correct outcome.' But the stated task domain has 'objectively verifiable outcomes.' A panel that rejects the decoy is demonstrating that it can identify objective irrelevance—i.e., that it already tracks the correct outcome. The 'justification delta' therefore measures outcome-tracking by requiring prior outcome-tracking, not independently of it. Under the target's own countercondition (decoy too obvious), the delta collapses to test-awareness; under a subtle decoy, the evaluator needs the ground truth to label it a decoy in the first place, so a 'reliable' panel's rejection is just re-deriving the answer the evaluator already has. There is no condition given where the decoy-rejection signal is both independent of the evaluator's ground truth and robust to obviousness, so the single-evaluation distinguishability claim is not substantiated.","counterexample":[{"decoy":"objectively irrelevant item","panel_loses_delta_without_ground_truth":true}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 purports to be a relation integrating SRC_001/SRC_002/SRC_004/SRC_005 into 'Verification Mode vs Obedience Mode,' but the stated refutation condition is not the negation of that integration: 'refuted if a panel can demonstrate genuine reliability gain through a justification delta without ever encountering or rejecting a decoy.' A genuine gain without decoy-rejection does not refute the existence or coherence of two modes; it only refutes a contingent sufficiency claim about decoys (SRC_001's mechanism), which is a component, not the integrated relation. The relation-as-stated can survive that outcome by holding that Verification Mode is realized by justification-delta evidence (SRC_005) even without decoy rejection. Thus the 'refuted if' is mis-specified relative to the claimed integration: it targets a sub-mechanism, not the covering principle, and a genuine relation should be refuted by failure of the dependence/reduction it asserts, not by viability of one diagnostic route.","counterexample":[{"panel":"demonstrates justification delta without decoy","relation_survives":true}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's 'justification delta' is vulnerable to its own countercondition (boilerplate justifications) in a way that defeats the single-evaluation goal: if the panel is trained to append plausible observations to each tick, then 'Item 4 is present because [Observation X]' will correlate with objective outcomes even when the reasoning is glib, because the checklist was designed around outcome-relevant items. The target says 'a high correlation between the justification and the objective outcome, independent of the checklist's phrasing' indicates genuine gain, but in a single evaluation with one panel and one outcome there is no variance to estimate such correlation or to separate trained boilerplate from genuine evidence. The proposed delta is therefore not identifiable from the single evaluation as required; it reduces to the same outcome-tracking it is supposed to diagnose.","counterexample":[{"panel":"appends boilerplate observation","correlation_with_outcome":true,"single_eval_identifiability":false}],"target_alias":"SRC_003"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 argues the expert wins because 'edge cases actually determine the objective outcome,' but this is only true for tasks where edge cases dominate. Its own counterconditions concede the expert loses on routine, finite-failure-mode tasks and when the expert is prone to lapse errors. Since the problem context fixes the comparison to 'tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes'—a class that includes both routine verifiable tasks (where checklists reduce lapses) and edge-case-dominated tasks—SRC_004's universal claim ('the single domain expert will remain more reliable') is not established for the stated scope. It has no derivation or evidence to show that edge cases determine outcomes across the whole class; it merely assumes a complex-task regime. For the routine verifiable sub-class, the mechanism predicts the opposite of the claim, so the claim overgeneralizes beyond the mechanism's domain.","counterexample":[{"task":"routine, finite failure modes, lapse-prone expert","checklist_panel_more_reliable":true}],"target_alias":"SRC_004"}]}